    {
      "id": "bad7fd3a-0a82-4fc1-b113-f075c0627e9c",
      "phaseId": "3e04a8f5-3c51-4f2d-8fab-d51b392e80bc",
      "number": 344,
      "shortId": "86ZPT",
      "priority": 6,
      "shortName": "action-task-e-an-action-declar",
      "title": "action-task-E — An action declares the arguments it takes",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "Handoff 2026-07-26, item 1. Same shape as the two defects fixed in F004/P011: something in the middle drops input and says nothing. There, a widget swallowed its children's events. Here, an action swallows its own arguments.\n\n## THE DEFECT\n\n`ActionMeta` (heca/src/actions.rs:1332) carries name, label, description, category, default_binding, icon, policy and confirm. It does **not** say which arguments the action takes. The only place that knows is the hand-written `match` in `build_action` (heca/src/input.rs:746-933), which reads each argument by literal string: `get_u64(args, \"pane_id\")`, `get_usize(args, \"ws_idx\")`, and so on across 35 arms.\n\nFour consequences, all silent:\n\n1. **A misspelled required argument kills the whole action.** `get_u64(args,\"pane_id\")?` returns `None`, so `build_action` returns `None`. From config (heca/src/app/registry.rs:35 `action_ref_from_config`) the binding then falls through to `ActionRef::Dynamic`, and at press time `dispatch_view_intent` (heca/src/app/interaction.rs:809) fails to resolve it and prints one line **only under `cfg(debug_assertions)`** (interaction.rs:828-835). In a release build the key does nothing, forever, with no output. README §\"Creating Custom Actions\" already documents this resignation: \"a typo is not an error: it simply never fires. If a binding seems dead, check the name.\"\n2. **A misspelled optional argument is worse — it silently takes the default.** `move_column`'s `focus` (input.rs:779), `take_pane`'s `focus_after` (832), `spawn_command`'s `float`/`close_pane`/`keep_on_error`/`keep_on_success` (912-928), `scrollback_line_up`/`_down`'s `amount` (850/853) all end in `.unwrap_or(...)`. Misspell one and the action runs with the wrong value and never complains, in any build.\n3. **An argument that does not exist at all is ignored.** Nothing compares the supplied names against a list, because there is no list.\n4. **23 of the 35 argument-taking actions have no metadata whatsoever.** Verified by comparing `build_action`'s arms against `ActionRegistry::ALL`: `focus_pane`, `focus_workspace`, `swap`, `move`, `move_pane_to_workspace`, `move_pane_to_column`, `move_column`, `swap_columns`, `resize`, `resize_to`, `float_at`, `close_pane_by_id`, `rename_target`, `rename_pane_by_id`, `rename_workspace_by_idx`, `rename_column_by_idx`, `reset_pane_name_by_id`, `reset_workspace_name_by_idx`, `take_pane`, `app_font_zoom`, `pane_terminal_font_zoom`, `add_column_to_workspace`, `spawn_command`. They are dispatchable by name from a widget, a menu, config and RPC, but `list-actions` / `describe-action` (heca/src/rpc.rs:117-140) cannot see them, they have no label, no icon, no declared policy. The only description of their arguments anywhere is a stale doc comment above `build_action` (input.rs:734-745) that lists 11 of 35.\n\n## WHAT TO BUILD\n\n**1. `ArgSpec` — a pure-data argument declaration** (heca/src/actions.rs, next to `ActionMeta`). Serializable, so it crosses to RPC and later to a plugin:\n\n```rust\npub struct ArgSpec {\n    pub name: String,\n    pub kind: ArgKind,          // Int | Float | Text | Bool | Enum(Vec<String>)\n    pub required: bool,\n    pub description: String,\n}\n```\n\n`ArgKind::Enum` carries the accepted spellings, so `resize`'s `target`/`axis`, `app_font_zoom`'s `step`, `spawn_command`'s `kind` and `chrome.container.move_to_region`'s `region` describe their own vocabulary instead of leaving it to `get_enum`'s silent `.ok()`.\n\n**2. `ActionMeta.args: Vec<ArgSpec>` — required, no default.** Same reasoning already written on `policy` (actions.rs:1322-1328): a default would mean \"the author forgot\" resolves silently to the most permissive setting. An action with no arguments declares an empty list, deliberately.\n\n**3. `ActionDescriptor.args: &'static [ArgSpec]`** for the built-in seed, and **descriptors for the 23 missing actions** so every dispatchable name has exactly one metadata entry. This is not scope creep: `ActionMeta.args` can only exist where an `ActionMeta` exists. Do NOT create a second table keyed by name — the \"two registries\" mistake is on record in this phase's description (the `DynActionMeta` incident). One catalog.\n\n**4. Delete `builtin_policy`'s hand-written fallback match** (actions.rs:1578-1600). It exists because a parameterized action has no bare-name `WmAction`, so it hand-picks a representative variant for `open_link`, `scroll_to_offset` and the four `chrome.container.*` ids, and `unreachable!`s on anything else — which is why the other 23 could never be added. With declared args the representative comes for free: build sample values from each `ArgSpec.kind` and call `build_action(name, &samples)`. That also makes a wrong declaration a **startup failure** (`with_builtins` runs at startup), not a latent one.\n\n**5. Check the arguments at the two doors, and say something.**\n   - `action_ref_from_config` (app/registry.rs:35): an unknown argument name or a missing required one is reported at load through the existing channel next to `log_conflicts` (app/registry.rs:92) — `eprintln!(\"[heca] …\")`, unconditional, not `cfg(debug_assertions)`. Config typos must be audible.\n   - `dispatch_view_intent` (app/interaction.rs:809): same check on the `Intent`'s args before building.\n   - Rule, matching the ViewNode model (docs/chrome-and-ui.md Part I §6, \"a single bad value costs only itself\"): an **unknown** argument is reported and ignored, the action still runs; a **missing required** argument is reported and the action does not run (today's behaviour, now audible). Never a crash — an unknown action name stays legitimate (a provider may not be mounted).\n\n**6. `ActionInfo` gains `args`** (actions.rs:1535) so `list-actions` and `describe-action` finally tell a caller what to pass. This is what F003/P001/T6 (generated plugin type definitions) and F003/P018 (command palette) both need and neither can get today.\n\n**7. Docs.** Delete the stale hand-written arg list above `build_action` — the data replaces it. Add the declaration step to README §\"Creating Custom Actions\" path A and to AGENTS.md \"Adding New Actions\", and correct the README line that tells the reader a dead binding is just something to live with.\n\n## TESTS\n\n- Round trip per declared action: build sample args from the declared `ArgSpec`s → `build_action` returns `Some`. Fails when a declaration names an argument the code does not read, or misses one it does.\n- Per required arg: drop it → `build_action` returns `None`. Per optional arg: drop it → still `Some`. This pins `required` honestly and catches the `.unwrap_or` arms being declared as required.\n- Every name `build_action` accepts has a catalog entry, and every catalog entry with a non-empty `args` is buildable.\n- A config binding with an unknown arg name reports and still binds; with a missing required arg reports and does not.\n- Put the original defect back (rename `pane_id` to `paneid` in one arm) and watch the tests fail, as was done for `pointer_delivery.rs` / `pointer_funnel.rs`.\n\n## GATE\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features` 0 warnings; `cargo test -p heca` green (937 across the workspace today). No `cargo fmt`. Nothing here is visual, so no in-app verification is needed — but the confirm dialog path must stay green since `ActionMeta` is shared with it.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\nDONE 2026-07-26 — branch `feat/action-arg-declaration`, 3 commits (dd350c5 code, 3963446 docs, + the Display test). NOT pushed, no PR yet.\n\n## WHAT SHIPPED\n\n**`ArgDescriptor` (static) / `ArgSpec` (owned) / `ArgKind` / `ArgProblem` / `check_args` — heca/src/actions.rs.** Same static/owned pair the file already uses for `ActionDescriptor` / `ActionMeta`, for the same reason: a plugin-registered action has no `'static` strings. `ArgKind` is `Int | Float | Bool | Text | Enum`; a vocabulary argument carries its accepted spellings.\n\n**`ActionDescriptor.args` and `ActionMeta.args` are required, no default.** A struct literal has to fill every field, so an action cannot be added without answering the question — the same argument already written on `policy`.\n\n**`EnumArg` trait — heca/src/input.rs.** `VALUES` lives beside each `FromStr` (`ResizeTarget`, `ResizeAxis`, `FontZoomStep`, `SpawnKind`, and `RegionId` in chrome/events.rs). A descriptor points at `<T as EnumArg>::VALUES` rather than restating the list, and `every_enum_arg_value_parses` walks each list through its own parser.\n\n**Both doors check and report.** `action_ref_from_config` → `log_arg_problems` (heca/src/app/registry.rs, unconditional `eprintln!`, next to `log_conflicts`); `dispatch_view_intent` → `report_arg_problems` (heca/src/app/interaction.rs). Missing required stops the action; unknown or malformed costs only itself. A misspelling offers the nearest declared name (Levenshtein, ≤ len/3).\n\n**`ActionInfo.args`** — `list-actions` / `describe-action` now say how to call what they list.\n\n## WHAT THE WORK TURNED UP, BEYOND THE TASK AS WRITTEN\n\n**Eight actions answered their bare name with every index set to 0.** `action_from_name` had `\"delete_workspace\" => DeleteWorkspace { ws_idx: 0 }`, and likewise `delete_column`, `add_pane_to_column`, `move_pane_to_workspace`, `move_pane_to_column`, `move_column_to_workspace`, `move_column`, `swap_columns`. Binding `delete_workspace` to a key deleted **workspace 0** — not the focused one, not nothing. Those arms are gone; the function's own doc comment had always said parameterized variants were not reachable from config, and now that is true. Checked first that no default binding and no menu entry relied on them (the sidebar menu supplies `ws_idx`/`col_idx`; the pane menu uses `add_pane_to_column` as an id only and runs `split_vertical`).\n\n**Five more actions were reachable only from RPC or the mouse.** `move_column_up`, `move_column_down`, `move_column_to_workspace`, `resize_column_by`, `resize_pane_height_by` had no descriptor, and the last three had no `build_action` arm either — so they could not be bound to a key or named by a menu or plugin, which is a P2 parity hole. All five are nameable now. `move_pane_left`/`move_pane_right` gained an optional `pane_id`, matching what their RPC commands always accepted.\n\n**23 argument-taking actions had no metadata at all** (the list in the original description). They have descriptors now — not scope creep: an argument list can only live where metadata lives, and there is one catalog.\n\n## TWO HAND-WRITTEN TABLES DELETED\n\n- `builtin_policy`'s representative-variant map. It named six actions and `unreachable!`d on the rest, which is exactly why the other 23 could never be catalogued. It now builds a stand-in from the action's own declared arguments via `ArgSpec::sample_value`, so a wrong declaration is a **startup panic**, not a latent bug.\n- Six entries of `test_descriptors_are_populated`'s UNBOUND list. An action with a required argument cannot carry a bare keybinding — a rule, not a list, and it exempts every future one for free.\n- Also gone: the stale doc comment above `build_action` that listed 11 of 35 actions' arguments.\n\n## TESTS (6 new + 1 refactor)\n\n- `every_declared_argument_is_read_by_the_action` / `every_required_argument_is_actually_required` / `every_optional_argument_is_actually_optional` (actions.rs) — walk every declaration, check `build_action` agrees.\n- `every_wm_action_variant_is_reachable_by_name` (input.rs) — the other direction: walks every `WmAction` and checks a declaration exists. Needed `each_variant()` lifted out of `test_action_priority_exhaustive` to module level.\n- `every_action_that_needs_a_target_refuses_to_default_it` — the line that stops the workspace-0 defect returning.\n- `check_args_names_what_is_wrong`, `a_misspelled_optional_argument_is_reported_even_though_the_action_still_runs`, `a_bindings_argument_mistakes_are_reported_at_load` (registry.rs), `a_problem_reads_as_a_sentence` (pins the wording, since printing is skipped under cfg(test) as conflict logging is), `introspection_carries_the_arguments`, `every_enum_arg_value_parses`.\n\n**Both original defects were put back and watched to fail.** `pane_id` → `paneid` in `build_action`'s `focus_pane` arm fails the round-trip test *and* stops the catalog building. A restored `\"delete_workspace\" => DeleteWorkspace { ws_idx: 0 }` fails the target test.\n\n## DOCS\n\nREADME §\"Creating Custom Actions\" step 6 (+ the `args` forms, the messages, the no-bare-keybinding rule); README §\"Actions System\" — the line saying a dead binding is just something to live with now distinguishes a wrong *name* (still resolved at press, still silent, and legitimately so) from a wrong *argument* (reported). AGENTS.md \"Adding New Actions\" step 8b + a warning on step 2 about placeholder fields in `action_from_name`. docs/widgets.md: introspection `args` payload, the Intent section, the `register_dynamic` example.\n\n## GATE\n\n`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features` 0 warnings. `cargo test --workspace` all green — 418 in heca (was 407), 20 suites. No `cargo fmt`. Nothing visual, so no in-app verification needed; the confirm-dialog path shares `ActionMeta` and stayed green.\n\n## NOT DONE, DELIBERATELY\n\n`parse_rpc_command` (heca/src/rpc.rs) is a **third**, positional argument parser, separate from `build_action`. It is not part of this defect — it already returns typed errors (`MissingArgument`, `ParseInt`) rather than failing silently. Folding it onto the declarations would let RPC report the same way and would remove the duplication, but it is a distinct piece of work with its own error-surface decisions.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
      "subtasks": [],
      "dependsOn": [],
      "startedAt": "2026-07-26T21:27:10.960Z",
      "completedAt": "2026-07-26T21:46:58.686Z",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-26T21:27:07.661Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-26T21:46:58.686Z"
    },
    {
      "id": "caa74f04-376d-480b-b096-dd8288b9566e",
      "phaseId": "99d19246-1ba9-4720-9895-e70c66acf144",
      "number": 345,
      "shortId": "D8PKQ",
      "priority": 18,
      "shortName": "a-property-value-can-hold-an-o",
      "title": "A property value can hold an object, so appearance is really authorable",
      "status": "done",
      "description": "# The hole\n\n`Visual` has six fields: `fill`, `border`, `glow`, `radius`, `font_size`, `font_scale`\n(`heca-grid-ui/src/style.rs:276`). **Two of them cannot be set from a description, and never could.**\n\n`border` is `Border { color, width }` and `glow` is `Glow { color, radius, intensity }`\n(`heca-grid-ui/src/scene.rs:271` and `:280`). A property value is a **closed list of scalars** —\n`Bool, Int, Float, Text, Size, Variant, Align, Color, Glyph, List`\n(`heca-view/src/lib.rs:218`). There is no object, so there is no way to write `{color, width}`.\n\n**F003/P017/T007 claimed otherwise.** Its commit `eb9e021` says \"the whole of `Visual` — fill,\nborder, glow, radius, font size, font scale\". It gave `Border` and `Glow` serde derives, which made\nthem look reachable. Serde on the type is not the same as a property value that can carry it.\nFour fields work; the commit says six. Nothing tested the two, so nothing failed.\n\nThis is the exact defect class F003/P017 existed to close, and it survived the phase.\n\n# Why this blocks F003/P011/T006\n\nThe typed builder SDK is meant to make a wrong property a compile error. Built on today's\n`PropValue` it would instead ship an exceptions list saying \"border and glow cannot be authored\" —\nfreezing the hole into a compile-checked API, which is precisely why T006 was split out of this\nphase in the first place (see its status log, 2026-07-25).\n\n# What to build\n\n**`PropValue::Map(PropMap)`** in `heca-view/src/lib.rs`. `PropMap` is already\n`BTreeMap<String, PropValue>` (`heca-view/src/lib.rs:260`), so the variant is recursive for free and\n**no future struct-valued field needs another variant** — that is the point. Serde is externally\ntagged with `rename_all = \"snake_case\"`, so it travels as `{\"map\": {...}}`.\n\n**`prop_to_json` must recurse** (`heca-view-realize/src/lib.rs:208`). It already recurses for\n`List` at `:226`. A `Map` becomes a JSON object, and — this is the part to get right — **a colour\ntoken nested inside it must still resolve**: `border = {color: \"accent\", width: 2}` has to go\nthrough `resolve_color` (`:221`, `:233`) at any depth, not just at the top level. Without that, a\nnested colour silently becomes the literal string \"accent\" and the field fails to deserialize.\n\n**Totality still holds.** `merge_style_half` (`heca-view-realize/src/lib.rs`) drops a value that\ndoes not fit its field *individually* — one bad property never discards the good ones and never\npanics. A malformed nested object must behave the same way; there is an existing test for the flat\ncase (`a_bad_colour_does_not_discard_its_neighbours`, `:1449`).\n\n**Not in scope:** `PropInput` (`heca-grid-ui/src/prop.rs:27`) stays `Bool | Number | Text`.\nThat is the *widget-property* channel, and no widget builder takes a struct today. `border`/`glow`\nare `Visual` fields, which reach the widget through `merge_style_half` and serde, not through\n`PropInput`. If a widget ever needs a struct property, that is a separate change — record it, do not\npre-build it.\n\n# Steps\n\n1. Add the variant + doc it as the extension point for any struct-shaped field.\n2. Recurse in `prop_to_json`, resolving colours at any depth.\n3. Tests in `heca-view-realize`: a described `border` reaches the widget; a described `glow` does;\n   a **token name** nested inside one resolves against the theme; a malformed nested object drops\n   only itself.\n4. **Fix the record.** T007's claim is repeated wherever appearance is documented — check\n   `docs/chrome-and-ui.md`, `AGENTS.md` and the `Visual` doc comments, and correct anything that\n   says all of `Visual` was reachable before this task.\n5. Then unblock F003/P011/T006 and let the SDK expose `border`/`glow` like any other property.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- A `ViewNode` can set `border` and `glow`, proven by a test that asserts the value on the realized\n  widget, not merely that it parses.\n- A theme token nested inside an object resolves.\n- Whole workspace green, clippy 0 warnings.\n\n---\n**Completion summary:**\n# ✅ DONE 2026-07-27 — commit `f2deecd`\n\n`PropValue::Map(PropMap)` in `heca-view/src/lib.rs`. Recursive, so **no future struct-shaped\nproperty needs another variant** — that was the point, not `border`/`glow` specifically.\n\n- `prop_to_json` (`heca-view-realize/src/lib.rs:208`) recurses, resolving a colour token **at any\n  depth**. Without that a nested `\"accent\"` reaches serde as the literal word and the whole field is\n  dropped, silently.\n- `prop_to_input` rejects `Map` alongside `List`, with the reason on the arm: `PropInput` is the\n  scalar channel for *widget* builders and no `#[prop]` builder takes a struct. `Visual` fields\n  reach the widget through `merge_style_half` and serde instead. If a widget builder ever takes one,\n  `PropInput` grows a variant then — deliberately, not pre-built.\n- Totality holds at depth: a member that does not convert costs only itself.\n\n**Three tests, asserting the value on the realized widget rather than that it parses**, and they\ninterlock: `a_bad_member_of_an_object_does_not_discard_the_rest` proves an unusable colour drops the\nborder; `a_token_nested_in_an_object_resolves_against_the_theme` proves `\"accent\"` yields the\ntheme's accent and follows a theme change. If resolution did not recurse, the second would take the\nfirst's path and fail. Plus\n`a_struct_shaped_appearance_property_reaches_the_widget` for border and glow together.\n\n**The record was corrected**, which was half the point of the task: `AGENTS.md:753` and\n`docs/chrome-and-ui.md` §R2 both repeated T7's \"the whole of `Visual`\". Both now state what was true\nthen, what is true now, and the lesson — **adding serde to a type is not the same as being able to\nauthor it; check the value channel.**\n\n964 tests, 0 failures, clippy 0 warnings. Nothing visual changed, so there was nothing for the user\nto look at; the proof is the tests.\n\n## Follow-up this exposed (not done here)\n\n`PropValue` carries three hardcoded enum mirrors — `Size`, `Variant`, `Align` — which is the same\nclosed shape `Map` was added to escape: every new enum would want its own variant. It does not need\none. Fixed value sets travel as their **name**, so a `Text` carries any of them and the *type* lives\nin the authoring layer. New mirrors added from here on must NOT get a `PropValue` variant. Collapsing\nthe existing three is a wire-format change and wants its own task.",
      "notes": "",
      "statusLog": [],
      "decisions": [],
      "acceptedDecisions": [],
      "checklist": [],
